<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sita</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sita is the heroine of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramayana">Ramayana</a>, wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> and daughter of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janaka">Janaka</a> of Videha, who in the epic found her in a furrow while ploughing. Her abduction by Ravana and her rescue drive the story. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> she is revered as an incarnation of the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lakshmi">Lakshmi</a> and an ideal of devotion, and Janakpur in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a> claims to be her birthplace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sita.jpg" alt="Sita" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ravi Varma Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sita_in_exile.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
